# Lists.
#
# Two primitives, and everything else a fold that carries its answer as it
# goes: `listAt` gives an `Option of T` and `listLength` gives a bound, so
# walking a list is an ordinary function whose last act is to call itself.
#
# WHAT MADE THE REST OF THIS FILE WRITABLE. `append item to list` — the
# construction form. `rest of` makes a list shorter, and until there was a
# way to make one longer no function could hand back a collection it had
# not been given, which is why `reverse` and `rest` were primitives and
# `split` and `values` still are in §17.4.10's table. They are not any
# more; the only two left here are the two that must be.
#
#   listLength, listAt   could be written now, over `rest of`, and must
#                        not be. Both are O(1) on the platform; written
#                        here each becomes O(n), and `listAt` is what
#                        §17.4.3 dispatches `at` to — so every index in
#                        every ZDeceptron program would go linear to
#                        remove one `foreign`.
#
# THE COST, PAID, TWICE. Every fold below used to recurse once per element
# with no accumulator, so its stack depth was linear in the length of its
# input and four thousand elements ran the host out of stack rather than
# returning. §17.4.10's local binding gives a fold somewhere to keep its
# answer, and the emitter turns a call in tail position into a jump,
# because no JavaScript engine does. Depth is now constant.
# `crates/zdc-codegen/tests/depth.rs` holds the numbers.
#
# The second cost is time, and it is why every fold below keeps an index.
# `rest of` copies, so a fold that consumes with it is quadratic: measured
# against this file, `sumOf` over ten thousand elements takes 7.3s written
# with `rest of` and 88ms written with `listAt` and an accumulator. The
# same arithmetic decides how a fold *builds*: `append` is O(1) and the
# chain it makes is flattened once, so a builder in tail position is
# linear — and only a builder in tail position is. `give append x to (f
# with …)` is not a tail call, so the chain is flattened once per element
# and the build is quadratic again. Every builder in this file is written
# in tail position.

foreign listLength is anywhere
    from "zd:list" as "length"
    takes of value is List of T
    gives pure Whole

foreign listAt is anywhere
    from "zd:list" as "at"
    takes value is List of T, index is Whole
    gives pure Option of T

function first of items
    give listAt with value is items, index is 0

function last of items
    give listAt with value is items, index is (listLength of items) - 1

function isEmpty of items
    give (listLength of items) is 0

# Everything but the first element, and `empty` when there is nothing to
# drop — so a fold that consumes with it needs no length check in front of
# it, only the `None` its `first of` gives back.
function rest of items
    give copyFrom with items is items, index is 1, taken is empty

# The elements of `items` from `index` on, appended to `taken` in order.
#
# The shape every builder in this library has: one element per step, the
# answer travelling as a parameter, and a call and nothing else at the end.
function copyFrom with items, index, taken
    when listAt with value is items, index is index
        None
            give taken
        Some with item
            give copyFrom with items is items, index is index + 1, taken is (append item to taken)

function reverse of items
    give reverseFrom with items is items, index is (listLength of items) - 1, taken is empty

function reverseFrom with items, index, taken
    when listAt with value is items, index is index
        None
            give taken
        Some with item
            give reverseFrom with items is items, index is index - 1, taken is (append item to taken)

# The target of `items contains value` when `items` is a List.
function listContains with items, value
    give listContainsFrom with items is items, value is value, index is 0

function listContainsFrom with items, value, index
    when listAt with value is items, index is index
        None
            give no
        Some with item
            if item is value
                give yes
            give listContainsFrom with items is items, value is value, index is index + 1

function sumOf of numbers
    give sumFrom with numbers is numbers, index is 0, total is 0

function sumFrom with numbers, index, total
    when listAt with value is numbers, index is index
        None
            give total
        Some with value
            give sumFrom with numbers is numbers, index is index + 1, total is total + value

# `parts` with `using` between them. O(n · p) in the total length n and
# the piece count p: each step concatenates its piece onto the whole of
# what is joined so far. The joined text travels as a parameter, so the
# last act is a call and the emitter turns it into a jump — the time is
# unchanged and the stack depth is one frame.
function join with parts, using
    give joinFrom with parts is parts, using is using, start is 0, index is 0, joined is ""

# `start` is the piece the walk began at, and it is carried rather than
# assumed to be 0 because `after` joins the pieces from index 1 onward.
# The separator goes *between* pieces, so the first piece taken must not
# have one in front of it — and "the first piece taken" is `start`, not
# necessarily the first piece of the list.
function joinFrom with parts, using, start, index, joined
    when listAt with value is parts, index is index
        None
            give joined
        Some with piece
            if index is start
                give joinFrom with parts is parts, using is using, start is start, index is index + 1, joined is piece
            give joinFrom with parts is parts, using is using, start is start, index is index + 1, joined is joined + using + piece

# `parts` joined with `using`, leaving the last piece out — which is what
# `beforeLast` is, once a `Text` has been split. O(n · p), like `join`,
# and like `join` it carries its answer so the last act is a call.
function joinAllButLast with parts, using
    give joinUntil with parts is parts, using is using, stop is (listLength of parts) - 1, index is 0, joined is ""

function joinUntil with parts, using, stop, index, joined
    if index >= stop
        give joined
    if index is 0
        give joinUntil with parts is parts, using is using, stop is stop, index is 1, joined is (valueOr with maybe is (listAt with value is parts, index is 0), fallback is "")
    give joinUntil with parts is parts, using is using, stop is stop, index is index + 1, joined is joined + using + (valueOr with maybe is (listAt with value is parts, index is index), fallback is "")

# CONSTRUCTION AT A LENGTH THE SOURCE DOES NOT WRITE OUT.
#
# Until `append` landed, a program could only have a collection of the
# size some literal in its own source spelled out, and the two functions
# below are that stopping being true. Neither needed a new word:
# `append` is the construction form and a self call in tail position
# is the loop, so a length that is a *value* is now as good as a length
# that is a literal. `map` still preserves length and `keep`/`take` still
# only shrink — what was missing was a first list of the right size to
# hand them, and that is all `indices` is.
#
# COST. Both are O(n) in the count and nothing worse. One `append` per
# element, `append` is O(1) because it links a chain node rather than
# copying (see the note on `copyFrom`), the chain is flattened once, and
# the recursive call is in tail position so the emitter turns it into a
# jump and the depth is constant. Written the obvious other way — `append
# index to (indicesFrom …)`, with the call inside the append — the chain
# is flattened once per element and the same loop is O(n²). The tail
# position is not a style preference here; it is the complexity.

# `[0, 1, … count - 1]`: the positions of a collection of that size.
#
# The one that unlocks the rest. `map each` binds the element and not its
# position, so a program that needs an index has to carry it in the datum
# — and this is where the datum comes from. `map each i to (tileAt of i)`
# over `indices of 960` is a grid, built at a size the program chose.
#
# A count of zero or less gives `empty`, so a caller need not guard.
function indices of count
    give indicesFrom with index is 0, count is count, taken is empty

function indicesFrom with index, count, taken
    if index < count
        give indicesFrom with index is index + 1, count is count, taken is (append index to taken)
    give taken

# `count` copies of `value`.
#
# `Array(n).fill(x)`, which is what four of the ported engines open with.
# A count of zero or less gives `empty`.
function filled with value, count
    give filledFrom with value is value, remaining is count, taken is empty

function filledFrom with value, remaining, taken
    if remaining > 0
        give filledFrom with value is value, remaining is remaining - 1, taken is (append value to taken)
    give taken

# --- folds that answer a question about a whole list ----------------------
#
# None of these takes a predicate, because the language has no first-class
# functions (§17.2). `anyOf` folds a list of `Truth` and the predicate is
# applied before the fold sees it:
#
#   from people map each person to person.active
#   ... then `anyOf of` that
#
# That is not a workaround for a missing feature. `map each` and `keep
# each` are how this language says "apply this to every element", and a
# prelude function that took a predicate would need a value no program can
# write.

# Whether any element is `yes`. Nothing is `no`, which is the identity of
# `or` and the answer that leaves the fold unchanged.
function anyOf of truths
    give anyFrom with truths is truths, index is 0

function anyFrom with truths, index
    when listAt with value is truths, index is index
        None
            give no
        Some with truth
            if truth is yes
                give yes
            give anyFrom with truths is truths, index is index + 1

# Whether every element is `yes`. Nothing is `yes`, for the same reason
# with `and` in place of `or`.
function allOf of truths
    give allFrom with truths is truths, index is 0

function allFrom with truths, index
    when listAt with value is truths, index is index
        None
            give yes
        Some with truth
            if truth is no
                give no
            give allFrom with truths is truths, index is index + 1

# How many elements are `yes`. `countOf of (map each x to p with x)` is
# "how many satisfy p", which is the aggregate most application code asks
# for and the one a pipeline cannot express on its own.
function countOf of truths
    give countFrom with truths is truths, index is 0, total is 0

function countFrom with truths, index, total
    when listAt with value is truths, index is index
        None
            give total
        Some with truth
            if truth is yes
                give countFrom with truths is truths, index is index + 1, total is total + 1
            give countFrom with truths is truths, index is index + 1, total is total

# The smallest and largest element, or `None` when there is no element.
#
# `Option` rather than a sentinel: nothing has no smallest member, and a
# sentinel is a lie that typechecks. Sorting and taking the first is the
# alternative, and it is O(n log n) for an O(n) question.
function minOf of numbers
    give minFrom with numbers is numbers, index is 0, best is None

function minFrom with numbers, index, best
    when listAt with value is numbers, index is index
        None
            give best
        Some with value
            give minFrom with numbers is numbers, index is index + 1, best is (smallerOf with best is best, value is value)

function smallerOf with best, value
    when best
        None
            give Some with value is value
        Some with current
            if value < current
                give Some with value is value
            give Some with value is current

function maxOf of numbers
    give maxFrom with numbers is numbers, index is 0, best is None

function maxFrom with numbers, index, best
    when listAt with value is numbers, index is index
        None
            give best
        Some with value
            give maxFrom with numbers is numbers, index is index + 1, best is (largerOf with best is best, value is value)

function largerOf with best, value
    when best
        None
            give Some with value is value
        Some with current
            if value > current
                give Some with value is value
            give Some with value is current

# --- taking a list apart by position --------------------------------------
#
# Both counts saturate rather than refusing, so paging past the end gives
# an empty page and a caller needs no guard in front of either. The names
# carry `list` for the same reason `listContains` does: `take` and `first`
# are keywords, and `dropFirst` already belongs to `text.zd`.

function listTake with items, count
    give listTakeFrom with items is items, count is count, index is 0, taken is empty

function listTakeFrom with items, count, index, taken
    if index >= count
        give taken
    when listAt with value is items, index is index
        None
            give taken
        Some with item
            give listTakeFrom with items is items, count is count, index is index + 1, taken is (append item to taken)

function listDrop with items, count
    give copyFrom with items is items, index is (max with first is count, second is 0), taken is empty

# `item` put at `index`, and whatever was there pushed along. An index at
# the length appends, which is the one position an insert has that a
# replacement does not.
function insertAt with items, index, item
    give copyFrom with items is (listDrop with items is items, count is index), index is 0, taken is (append item to (listTake with items is items, count is index))

# The list without the element at `index`. An index nothing occupies
# removes nothing.
function removeAt with items, index
    give copyFrom with items is (listDrop with items is items, count is index + 1), index is 0, taken is (listTake with items is items, count is index)

# The list with the element at `index` replaced. An index nothing occupies
# changes nothing, matching `removeAt`.
#
# `insertAt` and `removeAt` between them change a list's *length*; this is
# the one that changes its *contents*, and without it a table indexed by
# position cannot be updated in place at all (#195).
#
# The `when` is not a guard that could be dropped. Writing this as the
# obvious head-item-tail composition **appends** past the end, because
# `listTake` saturates: at `index` 5 of a one-element list the head is the
# whole list and the tail is empty, so the item lands on the end instead of
# nowhere. Asking `listAt` first is what makes an unoccupied index a
# no-op, which is the behaviour `removeAt` already has.
function setAt with items, index, item
    when listAt with value is items, index is index
        None
            give items
        Some with replaced
            give copyFrom with items is (listDrop with items is items, count is index + 1), index is 0, taken is (append item to (listTake with items is items, count is index))

# --- building a list ------------------------------------------------------

# Every list of `lists`, one after another, in order.
function flatten of lists
    give flattenFrom with lists is lists, index is 0, taken is empty

function flattenFrom with lists, index, taken
    when listAt with value is lists, index is index
        None
            give taken
        Some with items
            give flattenFrom with lists is lists, index is index + 1, taken is (copyFrom with items is items, index is 0, taken is taken)

# The elements with later repeats left out, keeping the first of each.
#
# Equality is `is`, the same equality `contains` uses. O(n²) in the number
# of distinct elements, because the only membership test available is a
# walk. A caller with a large list and an ordering should sort instead.
function withoutDuplicates of items
    give withoutDuplicatesFrom with items is items, index is 0, taken is empty

function withoutDuplicatesFrom with items, index, taken
    when listAt with value is items, index is index
        None
            give taken
        Some with item
            if listContains with items is taken, value is item
                give withoutDuplicatesFrom with items is items, index is index + 1, taken is taken
            give withoutDuplicatesFrom with items is items, index is index + 1, taken is (append item to taken)

# The whole numbers from `start` up to but not including `stop`.
#
# `indices of` is this with a start of zero, and it stays because every
# walk over a list uses it. A `stop` at or below `start` gives `empty`
# rather than counting down: one direction, one meaning, and counting down
# is `reverse of`.
function range with start, stop
    give rangeFrom with start is start, stop is stop, taken is empty

function rangeFrom with start, stop, taken
    if start < stop
        give rangeFrom with start is start + 1, stop is stop, taken is (append start to taken)
    give taken

# Each element of `left` beside the element of `right` at the same
# position. The shorter list decides the length.
#
# This had no return type to give until the language had a pair: two
# lists go in and a list of *two things at a time* comes out, and there
# was nothing to say that in. §17.7 recorded the same wall for combining
# two `Remote of T`s, which is one issue rather than two.
#
# `Pair of A to B` is what it gives, so `zip with left is names, right is
# scores` is a `List of Pair of Text to Whole` and each element answers
# to `.first` and `.second`. Equality is not among the things it answers
# to: `is` compares a pair by identity, so the checker refuses it exactly
# as it refuses a `List`, and `listContains` over a list of pairs is a
# type error rather than a wrong answer.
function zip with left, right
    give zipFrom with left is left, right is right, index is 0, taken is empty

function zipFrom with left, right, index, taken
    when listAt with value is left, index is index
        None
            give taken
        Some with here
            when listAt with value is right, index is index
                None
                    give taken
                Some with there
                    give zipFrom with left is left, right is right, index is index + 1, taken is (append (Pair with first is here, second is there) to taken)
